.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-drive-eject 1  "tapectl-drive-eject " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-drive\-eject \- Unload the cartridge from the drive, and tell the catalog it left
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl drive eject\fR [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Unload the cartridge from the drive, and tell the catalog it left
.PP
Reads the cartridge chip\*(Aqs serial (READ ATTRIBUTE through the SCSI generic node; no motion), then rewinds and unloads the cartridge (`MTOFFL`, what `mt offline` sends) — and records it: one contact (`drive eject`) naming the cartridge and the drive, and one `ejected` event on the cartridge (on the drive when the chip is not registered). It changes nothing else: the cartridge\*(Aqs volumes stay bound to it (an ejected cartridge is on a shelf, not displaced), and its place stays what `cartridge move` last said. When the cartridge is going somewhere else, the output names the command.
.PP
Refuses while any tapectl command holds the drive or a volume (a write, a verify, a restore, a poll): exit 75, naming the holder\*(Aqs pid and command, with nothing sent to the drive and nothing recorded. There is no `\-\-force`. Once it has the drive it holds the drive\*(Aqs lock until it exits, so no command can start a tape operation mid\-eject. The tape node being open in a process that is not tapectl is refused the same way (exit 75).
.PP
Strict, like every command that moves a cartridge: the drive must be a configured drive (`tapectl drive add`), whose SCSI generic node is checked to be the same drive as its tape node. On a machine with no configured drive, `mt \-f <device> offline` still works; the catalog is not told.
.PP
A catalog that cannot record the event after a good unload is a warning, not a failure.
.PP
Exit status: 0 = unloaded and recorded, or nothing to do because the drive was already empty (no contact, no event); 2 = not unloaded (the tape node could not be read, a cartridge is present and the drive is not ready, loading or faulted, or the unload failed: the contact is recorded as failed and there is no event); 75 = refused, a tapectl command or another process has the drive.
.PP
`\-\-dry\-run` does everything up to the unload: it checks for a holder, reads the node and the chip, and reports what it would eject; it takes no lock, sends no unload and records nothing. With `\-\-json` the whole of stdout is one JSON object.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
The drive to eject from, by its tape node path (default: the only one)

As configured (`[[backends.lto]].device_tape`; a by\-id link).
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format, errors included

Under it an error is one JSON document on stdout, `{"error": …, "exit_code": N}`, and the exit code is the command\*(Aqs own.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes

Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Debug tracing on stderr

Raises the log level to DEBUG (never lowers a `[logging] level` set higher); it never changes what a command does or prints on stdout.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr

Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmation prompts of risks you may accept

It never reaches a refusal: what is refused is a fact, not a risk to accept (ADR\-0008, Tier 3), and a command\*(Aqs own \-\-force names what it overrides.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs

The home is the first of: \-\-home; TAPECTL_HOME; the directory of \-\-config; this account\*(Aqs ~/.tapectl, when it exists; the home named by the home pointer /etc/tapectl/home (TAPECTL_HOME_POINTER names another file), when the account has no ~/.tapectl of its own; else ~/.tapectl, where `tapectl init` makes a new one. The config file is <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config names another. `tapectl status` and `tapectl config check` name the home in use and the rule that chose it.

As root the home pointer is never read, and on a host that has one tapectl refuses unless \-\-home or TAPECTL_HOME names the home: the service user\*(Aqs commands run through `tapectl\-op`.
